mod:list_ptr_from_call;
// A POINTER A CALL RETURNS INTO A `List` FREEZES WHAT HOLDS THE `List` (DEF-575, landing 177d2b3). A callee handed `@h` may hand back a pointer to
// anything `h` reaches, and its summary keeps no path for what it hands back, so the question is asked by type: may a `List`'s buffer under the
// place hold a value of the pointer's pointee type (`type_heap_holds`, alias.npk)? Where it may, the pointer is the element address `@l[0i64]` is
// (DEF-304, DEF-307; NITPICK-BORROW-015) in each place it can stand. Each module is one cell.
//   A POINTER HOLDER: `kc4`, `kc5` (a struct holding the `List`), `kc7` (through a named pointer), `kc9` (an array of them), `kn4` (an `any->`),
//     `kn5` (ten structs deep: the fuel's end answers yes), `kn6` (an array inside the element), `kr1` (a RECURSIVE struct: the pointee is the
//     place's own type, and it sits in the place's own `List`), `xa1` (a holder assigned after its declaration), `xg1` (a method's result),
//     `xh1` (a generic struct), `xw7` (a `wild` LOCAL the pointer was cast into), `xt2` (a generic struct's `List<T>`, read with the instance's
//     argument bound), `xm2` (a struct of another module with a field of a type private to that module, read at the struct's home).
//   AN AGGREGATE HOLDER over a place that HOLDS a `List` (before this row only a place that IS one was asked): `ka1`, `xe6`, `xe7` (a literal's
//     field), `xe1` (an `Optional`), `xe2` (an array), `xe3` (a `List` of pointers), `xe4` (a field assigned later), `xe5` (a struct the call itself
//     returned), `xw2` (a callee stores into the struct a pointer into the struct's own `List`), `xn2` (an enum's payload), `xn6` (a `dyn`).
//   A TEMPORARY, to the end of its full expression: `r3a`, `r3b`, `r3d`, `r3e`, `r3f`, `kr2` (a later argument replaces or grows it), `ri1` (an
//     ELEMENT's address handed through the call), `ri2`, `ri4` (the callee itself grows it through `@l`), `xf1` (a literal's later field grows it),
//     `xf2` (through a second call), `xf3` (in a `pass`), `xf4` (in a condition), `xf5`, `xf6` (a struct holding the pointer, as an argument),
//     `xg2` (a method's result), `xr1`, `xr2`, `xr3` (an element's address taken through a pointer to the `List` and handed through a call),
//     `xn4` (a struct holding the pointer, cast to `dyn`).
//   AN ASSIGNMENT'S TARGET, over its value: `k3b`, `k3g`, `xg3`; `k3h`, `k3j`, `k3l` (a `List` indexed through the call's pointer, so no name roots
//     the container and what the call reaches is frozen whole).
// Every cell above read or wrote freed heap on 177d2b2 (236, 230, 61, 60 or 67 by chance; the freed-heap detector's 97).
// `k3a`, `k3c`, `k3d`, `k3e` were NITPICK-ASSIGN-005 (and MOVE-001) already and carry both reports; `kc2`, `kc6`, `kt1`, `ri3`, `ka2`, `xe14`, `xw6`, `xx6`
// are the spellings the language refused before this row (a holder over a bare `List`, a named pointer to the inner `List` as the target's root,
// the explicit `@l[0i64]`, a `wild` or `wildx` field the pointer was cast into over a bare `List`).
// PRICES, right programs this refuses, each 67 under the detector on 177d2b2: `ok1`, `xe13` (a sibling field written while the pointer is held), `ok2`,
// `k3i`, `xe8` (a pointer to an INLINE field, which the type can't tell from an element), `xe12` (a write THROUGH an aggregate's pointer: a pointer in
// a field is not the holder's own, landing 165's reading, and `xe14` is its twin over a bare `List`), `krk` (an identity function over a recursive
// type), `k3n` (a sibling written beside a rootless target), `xr4` (a local pointer re-pointed inside the window), `xw2c` (the callee's store of
// `xw2` with nothing growing: the party is the struct's own, so the call that forms it is refused; `s.p = raw first(@s.l);` in the frame is the
// spelling that stands), `r3c`, `q8c`, `q46`, `q48` (read through before the later operand runs: the closed rule of DEF-410 (b)), `q23`, `q28`, `q29c`,
// `wq21c` (right by the callee's own order; the explicit `@l[0i64]` beside `@l` was refused already), `q47` (the inner buffer).
// The eight `q` cells stood in `tests/backend/programs/header_ptr_uses_ok.npk` until this landing, which re-spells them there over an array.
// A `wild` FIELD OF THE HOLDER IS NO POINTER MEMBER where the place isn't itself a `List`, because a `wild` slot takes a plain address only through
// `=>! wild T->`: without the cast it's NITPICK-BORROW-011, on each road measured: `xw8` (a store through a parameter), `xw9` (a literal's field),
// `xw10` (an assignment), `xw11` (from a plain local), `xw12` (through a `wild` parameter), and `xx8` to `xx12`, the same five over a `wildx` field.
// If one of the ten is ever accepted, that sentence is false and the line in `type_holds_elem_pointer` that rests on it goes. The plain party's
// question still counts a `wild` field (`xw13`: NITPICK-BORROW-013, a holder whose only pointer is one, against a move out of what it points at).
// `tests/backend/programs/list_ptr_from_call_ok.npk` is what stays legal beside these.
mod:kc4_holder_struct_replace = {
error:E5_kc4_holder_struct_replace;
struct:H = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; };
struct:HH = { H:h; int64:z; };
struct:SW = { wild int64->:p; int64:k; };
func:mk = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
func:mkh = H() never fails { pass H{ l: raw mk(), k: 2i64 }; };
func:eatl = int64(move List<int64>:v) never fails { pass 7i64; };
func:grow = int64(List<int64>->:p) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(p, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:refill = int64(List<int64>->:p) never fails { <-p = raw mk(); pass 7i64; };
func:reseth = int64(H->:w) never fails { <-w = raw mkh(); pass 7i64; };
func:newp = wild int64->() never fails { pass alloc(64i64) =>! wild int64->; };
func:first = int64->(List<int64>->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q)[0i64]); };
func:rd2 = int64(int64->:e, int64:z) never fails { pass <-e; };
func:fp = int64->(H->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:idp = int64->(int64->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:growh = int64(H->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:f = int64(int64:n) never fails {
    H:h = raw mkh();
    int64->:e = raw fp(@h);
    h = raw mkh();
    <-e = 7i64;
    pass h.l[0i64];
};
};
